✅ Step 1: Create EBS Volume (AWS Console)

Go to EC2 → Volumes

Click Create Volume

Select:

Type: gp3

Size: 20GB

Availability Zone: Same as EC2

Click Create Volume

✅ Step 2: Attach Volume to EC2

Select created volume

Click Actions → Attach Volume

Select EC2 instance

Device name: /dev/xvdf

Click Attach

(Inside Linux it appears as /dev/nvme1n1)

✅ Step 3: Connect to EC2

From laptop:

ssh -i ebs2.pem ec2-user@<public-ip>
✅ Step 4: Verify Disk
lsblk

You will see:

Root → /dev/nvme0n1

New Disk → /dev/nvme1n1

✅ Step 5: Create Partition
sudo fdisk /dev/nvme1n1

Inside fdisk:

n
p
Enter
Enter
Enter
w

Reload partition table:

sudo partprobe

Verify:

lsblk

Now you see:

/dev/nvme1n1p1
✅ Step 6: Format Partition
sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme1n1p1
✅ Step 7: Create Mount Directory
sudo mkdir /mnt/archana

(You can use any name like /mnt/data)

✅ Step 8: Mount the Volume
sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/archana

Verify:

df -h

You should see:

/dev/nvme1n1p1   20G   /mnt/archana
✅ Step 9: Create Test File
cd /mnt/archana
sudo touch f1
ls
✅ Step 10: Get UUID
sudo blkid /dev/nvme1n1p1

Copy UUID.

Example:

UUID="be8a1f6b-0ecc-4cbf-86d6-c2b5c4326c96"
✅ Step 11: Make Mount Permanent

Edit fstab:

sudo nano /etc/fstab

Add:

UUID=be8a1f6b-0ecc-4cbf-86d6-c2b5c4326c96   /mnt/archana   xfs   defaults,nofail   0   0

Save and exit.

Test:

sudo mount -a

If no error → correct.

✅ Step 12: Reboot and Verify
sudo reboot

Reconnect and check:

df -h
ls /mnt/archana

If f1 exists → ✅ Data persisted.

🧠 Super Short Memory Trick

Remember this order:

Create → Attach → lsblk →
fdisk → mkfs → mkdir →
mount → blkid → fstab →
reboot → verify
